<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Love song · c. 650 BCE · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Guan ju</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="lzh">關雎</bdi> / <bdi lang="lzh-Latn">Guān jū</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The first poem of China's oldest book of songs, from about 2,600 years ago. It begins with birds calling by a river.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous (Zhu Xi held it was composed by the ladies of the royal palace)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Zhou China</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classical Chinese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Zhou China, present-day China (&quot;Zhou nan&quot; section of the Airs of the States)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>seventh century BC</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Love song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous poem of the Classic of Poetry (7th century BC))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-ju">Guan ju</a>&quot; is the first poem of the Shi Jing, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>, and one of the best-known poems in Chinese literature. It has been dated to the seventh century BC, which makes it one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s oldest poems, though not the oldest in the anthology. It opens the &quot;Zhou nan&quot; section of the &quot;Airs of the States&quot;, the 160 poems that make up the larger part of the anthology's 305, and is typical of them: three stanzas of four-syllable lines. Its title comes from its first line, which calls up ospreys crying on a river islet. At heart the poem is about finding a good and fair maiden as a match for a young noble. Each stanza sets an image from nature beside the human scene without comment, a device early commentators called xing.</p><p>The earliest comment on it is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> praises it for joy without wantonness and sorrow without self-injury; Brooks and Brooks date that passage to 342 BCE, perhaps when the poem first came to prominence. A legend held that Confucius himself chose the songs of the Shi Jing from three thousand for their moral worth, and Confucian readers came to treat the poems as morally instructive. Three early schools, of Lu, Qi and Han, read it in different ways. The Lu school, probably dominant under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, took it as political criticism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-kang-of-zhou">King Kang of Zhou</a>, who was said to have risen late one morning; the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> wrote that it was composed when the house of Zhou was in decline. This reading lasted into the fifth century CE.</p><p>The Mao school, named after the early annotators Mao Heng and Mao Chang, prevailed and is the only tradition that survives whole. It read the poem as praise of Tai Si, queen of King Wen, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>, and its notes were edited by Kong Yingda in the seventh century as Mao shi zheng yi. Zheng Xuan, a century after the Maos, held that the young lady was not the queen but palace ladies whom the queen sought as further consorts for the king. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> Neo-Confucian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> largely agreed with the Mao school but thought the poem had been composed by the ladies of the palace. His contemporary Zheng Qiao called it simply an old folk song. A text found at Mawangdui in 1973, the Wuxingpian, reads it instead as a poem about bringing sexual desire into line with the customs of one's community.</p><p>Western scholars began to study the Shi Jing in the second half of the nineteenth century. Séraphin Couvreur, its first French translator, kept to the Mao reading, but James Legge, whose English translation appeared in 1871, rejected the Mao prefaces. Marcel Granet, in his 1911 study Fêtes et Chansons anciennes de la Chine, read it as a song of a rural courting festival; Qu Wanli saw a man about to be married, his bride greeted at the end with wedding music. Arthur Waley, C. H. Wang and Pauline Yu rejected the allegorical readings, while Edward L. Shaughnessy read the fish-eating osprey as an image of the search for a partner.</p><p>The poem has been alluded to throughout Chinese literature, one of the earliest times by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yong">Cai Yong</a> (died 192) in his &quot;Qingyi fu&quot;. Three of its phrases have become well-known four-character idioms (chengyu), still quoted in modern Chinese writing and speech, and the poem remains a common subject of study in Chinese studies.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A poem of courtship: a young noble's longing for a fitting bride, first unfulfilled and then fulfilled, set beside images from nature. Later readers have taken it as a wedding song, a festival courting song, a moral lesson on restraint, or praise of a virtuous queen.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Classical Chinese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Classical Chinese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">關關雎鳩，在河之洲。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Guān guān jūjiū, zài hé zhī zhōu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Guan, guan, cry the ospreys [?], on the islet in the River.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">窈窕淑女，君子好逑。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Yǎotiǎo shūnǚ, jūnzǐ hǎo qiú.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The modest and graceful good girl: a fine mate for the lord.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">參差荇菜，左右流之。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Cēncī xìngcài, zuǒ yòu liú zhī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Uneven grow the floating-hearts; to left and right one seeks [?] them.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">窈窕淑女，寤寐求之。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Yǎotiǎo shūnǚ, wù mèi qiú zhī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The modest and graceful good girl: waking and sleeping he seeks her.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">求之不得，寤寐思服。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Qiú zhī bù dé, wù mèi sī fú.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He seeks her and does not win her; waking and sleeping he thinks of her.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">悠哉悠哉，輾轉反側。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Yōu zāi yōu zāi, zhǎn zhuǎn fǎn cè.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long, oh long! He tosses and turns from side to side.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">參差荇菜，左右采之。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Cēncī xìngcài, zuǒ yòu cǎi zhī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Uneven grow the floating-hearts; to left and right one picks them.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">窈窕淑女，琴瑟友之。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Yǎotiǎo shūnǚ, qín sè yǒu zhī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The modest and graceful good girl: with zither and lute he befriends her.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">參差荇菜，左右芼之。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Cēncī xìngcài, zuǒ yòu mào zhī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Uneven grow the floating-hearts; to left and right one plucks [?] them.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="lzh">窈窕淑女，鐘鼓樂之。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="lzh-Latn">Yǎotiǎo shūnǚ, zhōng gǔ lè zhī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The modest and graceful good girl: with bells and drums he delights her.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Classic of Poetry (Shijing), Odes of Zhou and the South. Text from <a href="https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E8%A9%A9%E7%B6%93%2F%E9%97%9C%E9%9B%8E" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Hanyu Pinyin (tone marks, modern readings)).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'guan guan' imitates the birds' call. The bird 雎鳩 (jūjiū) is traditionally taken as an osprey or fish hawk; the identification is uncertain, hence [?]. 河 'the River' in early texts is the Yellow River.</li><li>line 1.2: 窈窕 (yǎotiǎo) is read as 'modest, retiring' or 'graceful, slender'; 君子 (jūnzǐ) 'lord, nobleman, gentleman'. 逑 'mate, match'; the variant 仇 means the same here. 好 read hǎo ('good'); some read hào ('loves').</li><li>line 2.1: 荇菜 (xìngcài) is a floating water plant, Nymphoides peltata (floating-heart). 流 literally 'flow, drift'; the Mao commentary glosses it as 'seek'; others read 'pick as they float'. Hence 'seeks [?]'.</li><li>line 2.3: 思服 'thinks of, keeps in mind' (服 glossed as 懷 'to cherish'). Variant 弗 for 不, same sense.</li><li>line 3.2: 琴瑟 (qín, sè) are two plucked zithers, the qin of 7 strings and the larger se; 'zither and lute' is a loose equivalent.</li><li>line 3.3: 芼 (mào) is glossed 'choose, pluck' or 'cook, prepare'; the variant 薅 means 'pull up'. Hence 'plucks [?]'.</li><li>line 3.4: 樂 read lè ('delight'); some read yuè ('make music for'). The subject 'he' is unstated in lines 2.2 to 3.4 (the gentleman of 1.2 is the usual reading).</li><li>rom: modern standard readings; old readings (e.g. 參差 cēncī, 芼 mào) as in dictionaries. No tone sandhi marked.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
